Yes. That distinction matters.

There is no reliable chemical or technique that simply flips a person into “truth mode.” Lowered inhibition is not the same thing as increased accuracy. Drugs, exhaustion, pressure, hypnosis, or suggestive questioning can all make someone talk more while also making memory noisier, confidence less trustworthy, and confabulation easier.

So the rule should be: disclosure ≠ truth, confidence ≠ accuracy, and altered state ≠ privileged access to reality.

I’ve added that as a standing epistemic safeguard.